The thing I love about your description is that Jonathan can't categorize the umbrella effect. That's much more interesting than simply saying “a strange-looking moon.” If he's a field researcher, he's looking at a phenomenon for which his existing vocabulary fails.
So I picture the observation progressing something like:
Planet Elsewhere: purple jungle, teal fungal growth, clouds, floating islands.
Sky: multiple moons.
Primary spectacle: one large moon apparently positioned in front of a smaller purple moon.
Anomaly: the large moon has an umbrella-like dissolution around it—except it isn't actually disappearing. The boundary seems to be simultaneously coming apart and remaining intact.
And then, behind all that: two more moons, distant enough to establish that this isn't some ordinary optical trick involving two objects.
That's fantastic.
Because now there's a question that didn't exist five minutes ago:
Is the “umbrella” actually part of the moon?
Or is it something between the moon and the observer?
Or—my favorite possibility—is the moon somehow being seen in two states at once?
Jonathan has no instrument capable of answering that yet.
And I think that's exactly the sort of discovery that makes Elsewhere worthy of its name. 🌌
The really delicious part is that all of this apparently came from “anywhere.”
